what is going on everybody and welcome  to a tech review of some security  cameras from real link so recently as  some of you guys know I moved and I was  in the market for some more security  cameras I was doing a bunch of research  I was just like completely blown away by  the number of options that are available  and trying to figure out which one to  get bla bla and I get asked to review  things all the time  but I don't think I've ever been asked  to review security cameras but I was by  this guy right as I'm researching them  and the timing was just too perfect so  here we are with a review now the camera  itself is an RLC 410 from rio link and  these are actually coming in a full  system so the system that i have here is  the rlk 8 - 410 b4 basically it's just  for the RL RL c 4 tens with a 2 terabyte  DVR which you don't necessarily have to  get those exact things so what are these  these rpoe or power over ethernet  cameras they are indoor/outdoor cameras  wherever you wanted to put them and they  also have infrared so you can see at  night so that actually you can see all  the little infrared LEDs there so that's  actually quite a few LEDs so let's  should get us some pretty good distance  and pretty good coverage the other  cameras that I have I want to say just  have like four LEDs and that actually  gets quite a bit of coverage so it'll be  interesting to see how good these are so  I've got four of these cameras to play  with here and so we need to talk about  what p OE or power over ethernet is and  i think you know really there's like  three main categories you could probably  make a four you could really make a  fourth for sure with cameras but you've  got like fully wired let's say so p OE  is fully wired right you've got the  camera has to be fully wired all the way  to the central base then you've got like  Wi-Fi type of cameras which just need to  be and these are generally called  wireless cameras these are going to be  they  still have wire for power but they  transmit data wirelessly and then you  have what are being called wire free  cameras which really just means  battery-powered now I'm not the biggest  fan of battery-powered cameras you know  the whole the way they work first of all  is offsetting batteries but they're not  always recording they only record events  like motion detected events or passive  IR detected events which I'm just not  the biggest fan of but they have their  various use cases for sure but I'm more  after something that's just recording  24/7 so then that pretty much throws me  into the category of the wireless or  Wi-Fi cameras versus fully wired now I  already have wireless cameras those are  really nice for when you you you have  like a good distance from your main base  unit but you're close to a plug that you  could plug in empower them so they have  their use but the problem is they still  lose their signal from time to time I  mean like just like the wind blowing can  make the signal go away  and so that's kind of annoying so with p  OE you just have high fidelity like it's  gonna work or any fully wired camera you  can have a little more trust in faith in  the system 99% of what I use cameras for  is like bing-bong the doorbell rings I  just want to I always want to look right  over to my monitor and see what's going  on so and yes I know that they have all  kinds of things that you can just like  put up there the problem is then you  gotta like open up your phone or  whatever and I just I want to literally  be able to just look instantly and get  my answer  so anyways not to say there's not places  for like the battery powered stuff or  you know any other options out there it  just kind of depends so in my case I've  got a garage that is unfinished so  there's no insulation on the walls and  there's really just plywood for the  ceilings but being able to run the cords  will be super super simple here also the  best deterrents there could possibly be  so in here we got basically four of  these I think they're about 25 feet but  don't quote me on that we got four  cameras DVR mouse for the DVR and  somewhere in there should be like an  HDMI cable and all that so now what I'm  going to do is going to go ahead and  pull away all this stuff and start  plugging it in it doesn't come that  messy by the way I'd already opened it  up so far setting up super simple just  plug and play  [Music]  this is one of the best tools ever made  on the camera there's like three a two  millimeter allen screws the each camera  comes with a supplied two millimeter  allen wrench you can turn all those to  adjust rotation and all that so to  adjust the camera all I did was I just  used the real link app on my phone and  then I you know adjusted the camera to  exactly where I wanted it angle wise and  all that so that was actually super darn  simple too to get everything set up now  did mention before that you could in  theory you know you you could use a POA  camera and not actually wire it all the  way to a central unit and so this  picture is just a quick example of a way  that I tested to make sure that I was  correct there and sure enough if you  wanted to you could have a POS which so  in this case I'm just using a Netgear  POS which connected to a Google Wi-Fi  router and then obviously power the  switch and then plug the camera into it  and that works totally fine  interestingly enough if it's on the same  network as the NVR the NVR actually  picks it up and you could record  straight to the NVR but you can also do  all the other things that you can do so  this is just an image of the night shot  from the app on my phone the app again  super super simple to set up you just  have to log in that's it took me like I  don't know a minute to get everything  set up so that was really impressive  this is of the backyard the infrared it  might be kind of hard to tell but that's  actually a pretty good distance there so  the distance from where I'm showing this  image now and again this image is an  image off of my phone and I didn't  realize it at the time but you can  actually set the quality to be full  quality so this is a degraded quality in  the name of one not using up a bunch of  you know phone data but also to stream  it very very smoothly but when it  records it records in high definition  anyways the distance there to the door  is about 73 feet I measured it and then  to the corner there is 85 feet so if  everything was in your favor you might  get the the up to 100 foot but this is  actually pretty darn good this is much  better than my other infrared cameras  so then I've moved it a little bit and  just to show you so the leaves there  that are you know real bright those are  25 feet away so that's that's pretty  good because I call you know I turn off  all the lights and all that most of the  time you see infrared you know examples  of IR cameras they generally have a  light source that is at least aiding  them and in this case there was no light  source even the reflection that you see  there that's the infrared so anyways I'm  pretty impressed and finally onto the  real link software if the phone app was  any indication it's no surprise that the  desktop app is just as simple to use has  quite a bit more features though so  first of all you can obviously set up  you know all kinds of different you know  organizations of the cameras that you  want to go with also the well first of  all I'm only showing two cameras thought  it was kind of stupid to show like all  of the cameras so anyway just the two  and the reason why this is getting  duplicated interestingly enough is B  this is the camera that I'd showed just  moments ago with that's connected only  via the Google mesh networking the  Google Wi-Fi router so that's pretty  cool it's it's it's being it's being  shown twice because it's being picked up  as a net like a camera on the network  but also the NVR is picking it up  because it's on the same network I  didn't think that would happen I thought  you would have to wire the camera to the  NVR if you wanted to record on to it  that's not the case so that's pretty  cool so anyways you can change the  layout all that you can double click on  one of them and yeah so now you've got  basically like the full resolution of  that camera it looks pretty good and you  can do all kinds of stuff so first of  all we could go into the settings for  the actual desktop itself we could set  up various rules and stuff for  downloading and capturing things like  from your cameras to your local machine  I wouldn't recommend it unless you've  got giant hard drives though but then  you can also set up the NVR itself by  clicking on the Settings here and then  again same things you can do various  schedules so right now this is just a  24/7 schedule but I could you know knock  out a block here or something like that  if I wanted to or I could say you know  what in this time range I actually  record but only if you see motion or  something like that you can do so that's  pretty cool and again super intuitive  just to set that up and get it  structured how you want also besides the  recording to your PC you also can  actually record the cameras to FTP so if  you're really security conscious or  you're really worried that you're gonna  get broken into or whatever and you want  to save the evidence that's the best way  to do it a lot of times if they're gonna  break in they're gonna want to find out  where it's getting recorded to but if  it's going to FTP good luck  so anyways also you can go to playback  you can playback historically and if  you're recording on motion or there's  motion detected you could very quickly  find that otherwise like I'll go to the  NVR I'll choose channel two and I can  see down here it's kind of in the way  but I can see down here all the dates  that there is something so this has only  been connected well this has been  connected as of yesterday to the  specific spot so what we could do is I  could just go over here all the other  time it's just a micro I was headed in  my garage and we can hit play here oh I  probably need to bring no it's there  okay  so again you can see the night vision  but it's getting a lot of reflection  both from the porch light here but then  also here so as far as night vision I'd  probably want to like angle it maybe  away from the tree or something like  that or maybe cut away some more of  these leaves so we're not getting that  reflection back to the camera  anyway that's good let's try to find  some daylight here there we go beautiful  amazing okay so the next thing is like  let's say oh I see something or I want  to download it or whatever you can just  come up here and hit this download and  then basically you can download it in  like our chunks you can download  multiple chunks if you wanted or all the  chunks if you want it but it's basically  separated in the little hour chunks for  you hit download and it'll go to your  download directory so anyways I believe  that covers everything with the cameras  overall I'm super impressed this was one  of the easiest ones to set up I was a  little worried with you know having to  run the wires but again you could use a  switch but also a real link does produce  Wi-Fi camera so if that's what you're  really going after then use it although  I would say if you do use Google Wi-Fi  you know how good it is so  I would probably rather use that than  the Wi-Fi camera I haven't used theirs  but the other Wi-Fi cameras I have I  don't like them as much because they  lose signal fairly frequently especially  because it's going through like brick  walls and many other walls to get to a  central unit anyway that's it I'm super  impressed I really like these cameras if  you're looking for you know POA cameras  I would HIGHLY highly recommend these if  you have any questions comments whatever  on the cameras feel free to leave them  below otherwise I will see you guys next  time

